.\" logis(1): hand-written roff (man(7) macros only); no build step.
.\" View with: man -l docs/man/logis.1
.\" The caller contract here matches README.md's "Caller contract" section,
.\" which names the test that pins each statement. Change both together.
.TH LOGIS 1 2026-10-16 logis "User Commands"
.SH NAME
logis \- a cogitator of the Machine God: Wayland launcher and dmenu\-compatible picker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logis
.RB [ \-\-apps ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B logis
.BR \-\-dmenu " | " \-\-dmenu0
.RI [ RUNE ...]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B logis
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.B \-\-provider
.I PROGRAM
.RI [ ARG ...]
.br
.B logis
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.B \-\-servitor
.br
.B logis \-\-check\-config
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B logis daemon
.RB [ \-\-idle\-timeout
.IR SECONDS ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.br
.B logis \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logis
raises a cogitator: a GPU\-rendered window on the compositor's overlay layer
(wlr\-layer\-shell) in which the user filters a list by typing and chooses
one row.
It launches desktop applications, serves scripts as a drop\-in for
.BR "fuzzel \-\-dmenu" ,
shows what external
.I providers
say, and drives
.BR servitor :
its rites, their aspects and inscriptions.
.PP
One binary plays two roles.
The
.I daemon
(the machine spirit) is resident: it holds the Wayland connection, the GPU
and the fonts warm, and listens on
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/logis.sock .
Every other invocation is a
.IR client :
it connects to the daemon, starting one
.RB ( "logis daemon \-\-detach" )
when none answers, opens one
.IR session ,
and waits for its answer.
A daemon a client starts outlives that client and serves later ones.
.PP
To only make sure a daemon runs, without opening a window, start it yourself:
.B "logis daemon \-\-detach &"
(it exits 0 at once if a daemon already answers).
.SH APP LAUNCHER
Plain
.BR logis ,
or
.BR "logis \-\-apps" ,
opens the app launcher, as fuzzel does: the desktop applications of
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/applications
and the
.I applications
directories of
.BR $XDG_DATA_DIRS ,
those used most and lately first, with their icons.
Typing filters them by name, generic name, keywords and program;
.B Return
launches the selected one and exits 0;
.B Shift+Return
opens its desktop actions in the same window.
.PP
Plain
.B logis
is a tabbed launcher: two plates above the
panel,
.B Applications
and
.B Rites
(servitor mode's rites), each counting what the query matches.
.B Tab
or
.BR Shift+Tab ,
or a click on a plate, switches tabs; the query stays.
.B Return
acts in the shown tab: an application is launched, or a rite leads to
servitor's invocation; either exits 0.
It opens on the tab last used, kept in
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/logis/tab .
.B "logis \-\-apps"
and
.B "logis \-\-servitor"
are the single lists as before and leave the remembered tab alone.
Without
.B servitor
on the
.B PATH
of whatever started plain
.B logis
(a compositor keybind's
.B PATH
can differ from the shell's), the strip shows all the same and the rites are
silent: their plate counts a dash instead of rites, their tab says that
servitor cannot be found and why, and
.B Return
there does nothing.
Hidden are entries with
.BR NoDisplay ,
.BR Hidden ,
an
.BR OnlyShowIn / NotShowIn
that excludes the current desktop, or a missing
.BR TryExec .
An app starts focused (xdg\-activation), in the session environment (the
systemd user manager's, else the caller's), in a transient systemd scope of
its own where one is available, and outlives logis;
.B Terminal=true
apps run behind the configuration's
.BR terminal ;
without one, behind the caller's
.BR "$TERMINAL \-e" ;
without that,
.B xdg\-terminal\-exec
on the caller's
.BR PATH ;
else the first of
.BR foot ,
.BR kitty ,
.BR "alacritty \-e" ,
.BR "wezterm start \-\-" ,
.BR "ghostty \-e" ,
.BR "konsole \-e" ,
.BR "gnome\-terminal \-\-" ,
.B "xfce4\-terminal \-x"
and
.B "xterm \-e"
found on the caller's
.BR PATH ,
searched afresh at each launch.
With none, the error says to set
.B terminal
in the configuration file the daemon reads.
.SH DMENU MODE
.B logis \-\-dmenu
reads items from stdin, one per line
.RB ( \-\-dmenu0 :
one per NUL\-ended record, which may contain newlines).
stdin is read as it arrives: the window opens before the input ends, and
logis may answer and exit before reading all of it, so a producer may meet a
closed pipe.
.PP
Typing filters the items: fuzzy matching that ignores case and accents, with
fzf's syntax
.RB ( ^prefix ,
.BR suffix$ ,
.BR \(aqexact ,
.BR !not ,
words matched separately).
Return prints the selected item's original bytes, exactly as read, whatever
their encoding, followed by a newline, and exits 0.
When nothing matches, Return prints the typed text as typed (unless
.BR \-\-only\-match ).
stdout carries nothing but the choice and its newline, also with
.BR \-\-dmenu0 .
.SH RUNES
A rune's value follows it as
.BI \-\- rune = V
or
.BI \-\- rune " V"
.RB ( \-\-password
takes its character only as
.BR \-\-password=\fIC\fP ).
The list runes need
.B \-\-dmenu
or
.BR \-\-dmenu0 ;
given without, they are an error.
An unknown rune, or a value a rune cannot bear, is an error naming it.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dmenu
Items are separated by newlines.
.TP
.B \-\-dmenu0
Items are separated by NUL.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prompt =\fITEXT\fP
Shown before the query.
Default:
.RB \(dq "> " \(dq.
.TP
.BI \-\-prompt\-only= TEXT
A prompt and nothing else: stdin is not read and no rows are shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-placeholder= TEXT
Shown while the query is empty.
.TP
.BR \-\-password [ =\fIC\fP ]
Shows each typed character as
.I C
(default
.BR * ).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lines =\fIN\fP
Rows shown, and so the window's height: the window is exactly as tall
as its prompt line and
.I N
rows (on an output too short for them, as many as fit, spread over the
output's height).
Default: the theme's
.B window.rows
(eleven for the bundled themes, 820 logical pixels wide).
A theme's
.B window.height
(an older key) is checked and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width =\fIN\fP
The window's width in characters of the theme's row font (1 or more; an
estimate).
Default: the theme's window width.
.TP
.B \-\-counter
Shows how many items match, of how many.
.TP
.BI \-\-search= TEXT
The query the window opens with.
.TP
.BI \-\-select= TEXT
Preselects the first item equal to
.IR TEXT ,
ignoring case.
.TP
.BI \-\-select\-index= N
Preselects item
.I N
(counted from 0).
Not together with
.BR \-\-select .
.TP
.B \-\-only\-match
Return accepts only an item, never typed text.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty
When stdin is empty, exit 1 at once: no window, no daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-index
Prints the chosen item's index (counted from 0) instead of its text.
.TP
.BI \-\-with\-nth= N\fR|\fPFORMAT
Shows column
.I N
(or a format) of each item; the whole item still comes back.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept\-nth= N\fR|\fPFORMAT
Prints column
.I N
(or a format) of the chosen item.
.TP
.BI \-\-match\-nth= N\fR|\fPFORMAT
Matches the query against column
.I N
(or a format) instead of what is shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-nth\-delimiter= C
The column delimiter, one ASCII character.
Default: tab.
.TP
.BI \-\-config= PATH
The configuration a daemon that this client starts reads.
A daemon already running keeps its own.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignored (dmenu's case switch; logis always ignores case).
.PP
Columns count from 1.
A
.I FORMAT
is free text in which
.BI { N },
.BI { N .. M }
and
.BI { N ..}
expand to columns, ranges joined by the delimiter;
.B 0
means the rune is not given.
.SS fuzzel compatibility
logis takes fuzzel's dmenu runes (fuzzel(1) 1.15) and its exit statuses 0 and
1.
Where fuzzel's documentation leaves a choice open, or logis differs on
purpose:
.IP \(bu 2
typed text is printed as typed, even with
.B \-\-index
or
.BR \-\-accept\-nth ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-select
wants an item equal to its text ignoring case, not a prefix or a fuzzy match,
and applies when that item arrives, until the user moves the selection or
edits the query;
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty
exits 1 (nothing was chosen);
.IP \(bu 2
a format that names no column is an error, not a constant output;
.IP \(bu 2
list runes without
.B \-\-dmenu
are an error;
.IP \(bu 2
errors exit 2, apart from a dismissal's 1, where fuzzel exits 1 (its
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
for one);
.IP \(bu 2
Shift+Insert pastes the clipboard, not the primary selection; the middle
button pastes the primary selection.
.SH PROVIDER MODE
.B logis \-\-provider
.I PROGRAM
.RI [ ARG ...]
runs
.I PROGRAM
with everything after
.B \-\-provider
as its arguments.
The daemon starts it as this client would have (its working directory and
environment) and shows the stages it sends: lists in one window, whose items
may be pending, with toasts.
The provider speaks the provider protocol, version 1: JSON lines over its
stdin and stdout, described in
.I docs/provider\-protocol.md
of the source.
.PP
The client exits 0 when the provider sends
.BR close ,
1 when the user dismisses the window or another interactive session replaces
it, and 2 when the provider cannot start, breaks the protocol, shows no stage
within 5 seconds, or exits before
.BR close .
When the session ends without its
.BR close ,
the provider is sent
.BR cancel .
A provider that was never sent a choice is then ended; one that was may be
carrying out an action and is left to finish it.
.SH SERVITOR MODE
.B logis \-\-servitor
lists every rite of
.B servitor census \-\-vigil
at once, with the aspect each stands in and its purpose.
A rite whose auspex has not settled yet is drawn pending and cannot be chosen;
it settles in place.
Choosing a rite shows its aspects, the current one marked and selected only
once settled (an edict has none); then one prompt per inscription the rite
declares, its decree for the chosen aspect already typed in, a mandatory one
.RI ( key *)
needing a word.
Then logis starts
.B servitor invoke
.I rite
.RI [ aspect ]
.RB [ \-\- \fIkey\fP
.IR word ]...
detached, in a session of its own with stdin, stdout and stderr on
.IR /dev/null ,
and closes at once.
It never waits for the invocation, reads its outcome or signals it; servitor
announces the verdict itself.
.B servitor
is found on the caller's
.BR PATH .
.PP
The client exits 0 once the invocation is started, 1 when the user dismisses
the window or another interactive session replaces it, and 2 on error.
Troubles of logis's own (no servitor, a census that breaks off, a heretical
rite) show as a toast in the window.
servitor mode is a provider session whose provider is logis itself
.RB ( "logis provide servitor" ,
not meant to be run by hand), so the rules of
.B PROVIDER MODE
hold.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Return\fR, keypad \fBEnter\fR, a click on a row
Accept.
.TP
.B Shift+Return
In the app launcher, open the selected application's desktop actions;
elsewhere the same as
.BR Return .
.TP
.B Escape\fR, \fBCtrl+G\fR, \fBCtrl+[\fR, losing keyboard focus
Dismiss (focus loss only while the configuration's
.B focus\-loss
is
.BR dismiss ,
the default).
.TP
.B Up\fR, \fBShift+Tab\fR, \fBCtrl+P\fR / \fBDown\fR, \fBTab\fR, \fBCtrl+N
Select the previous / next item.
In plain
.BR logis 's
tabbed launcher (see
.BR "APP LAUNCHER" ),
.B Tab
and
.B Shift+Tab
switch tabs instead.
.TP
.B Backspace\fR / \fBDelete
Delete the character before / after the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W\fR, \fBCtrl+U
Delete the word before the cursor; clear the query.
.TP
.B Left\fR, \fBRight\fR, \fBHome\fR, \fBEnd
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V\fR, \fBShift+Insert\fR; the middle button
Paste the clipboard; paste the primary selection.
.SH SESSIONS
Each client holds one session, on its own connection to the daemon, and the
answer goes to that client alone.
At most one session is visible.
.TP
.B dmenu clients queue.
A dmenu session that arrives while another session (of any kind) is visible
waits, and the queue is shown first in, first out; each client gets its own
answer.
A queued client waits as long as the sessions ahead of it take; to bound
that, run it under
.BR timeout (1).
.TP
.B Interactive sessions replace.
An app, servitor or provider session takes the visible session's place, and
that session's client exits 1.
Queued dmenu clients stay queued and are shown once the interactive session
ends.
.TP
.B Leaving cancels.
A client that exits or is killed (by
.BR timeout (1),
Ctrl+C, \&...) cancels its session: a visible one closes and the next queued
one is shown; a queued one is dropped.
.PP
Connections are not ordered among themselves.
The queue follows the order in which the daemon
.I receives
sessions, not the order in which clients were started: two clients started a
moment apart may be answered in either order, and a session opened just
after another client left may wait in the queue until the daemon has handled
that departure.
Clients that need an order should run one after another.
.SH DAEMON
.B logis daemon
is the daemon itself, in the foreground; clients start it as
.BR "logis daemon \-\-detach" ,
which stops writing to the starter's stdout and stderr once it listens.
It checks the Wayland session and the configuration at start, and exits 2 when
either is unusable or when the compositor goes away.
A second daemon finds the first one listening and exits 0.
.PP
A daemon left running across an upgrade keeps serving, but once its binary was
replaced since it started, every window it shows carries a notice that logis
was updated and the daemon wants a restart, naming
.B "systemctl \-\-user restart logis"
when systemd passed it the socket, or
.BI "kill " PID
otherwise.
Every client prints the same line on stderr (its exit status and stdout are
unchanged), and the daemon's log has it once.
The daemon never restarts itself.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle\-timeout " SECONDS"
Exit once no client has been connected for that long; 0 keeps the daemon
resident.
Outranks the configuration's
.BR idle\-timeout ;
off by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
The configuration to read instead of the default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.BR \-\-dmenu :
an item or typed text was accepted; it is on stdout.
.BR \-\-provider :
the provider closed its flow.
.BR \-\-servitor :
.B servitor invoke
was started.
Plain
.B logis
or
.BR \-\-apps :
an application was launched (in plain
.BR logis 's
tabbed launcher, also: a rite was invoked).
.B "logis daemon \-\-detach"
when a daemon already answers.
.BR \-\-check\-config :
the configuration is sound (or absent), and a custom shader it names compiles.
.BR \-\-version :
the name and version are on stdout.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing was chosen, and stdout is empty: the user dismissed the window,
another (interactive) session replaced it, or
.B \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty
met an empty stdin.
.TP
.B 2
An error; stdout is empty and the message is on stderr.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Whenever logis cannot serve, it exits 2 with an empty stdout and the reason
on stderr.
The message is for people, flavoured, and its wording may change; scripts
should branch on the exit status alone.
It does name the plain fact:
.TP
no Wayland session
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is unset (the message says so), or its socket cannot be reached (the message
names its path).
.TP
no layer shell
The compositor (GNOME/Mutter, for one) offers no
.BR zwlr_layer_shell_v1 ,
named in the message.
A compositor without
.B wp_fractional_scale_manager_v1
is refused likewise.
.TP
no runtime directory
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset or not absolute.
.TP
an invalid configuration
The daemon refuses to start; the message names the file, line and column.
.TP
the daemon died during the session
For instance because the compositor left; the message says the daemon fell
silent.
A daemon that lost its compositor removes its socket, and the next client
starts a new one.
.TP
the daemon refused the client
A daemon of another protocol version, left running across an upgrade, refuses
the client with its reason: restart that daemon
.RB ( "systemctl \-\-user restart logis"
where
.B logis.socket
is enabled; otherwise end it, and the next client starts a new one).
.PP
Every client exits 2 in each case that stops a daemon starting, so a script
can let the exit status decide and fall back to another picker:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
items=\(aqrite of oil
rite of ignition\(aq
choice=$(printf \(aq%s\en\(aq "$items" | logis \-\-dmenu)
case $? in
0) printf \(aqchosen: %s\en\(aq "$choice" ;;
1) ;;   # nothing chosen: dismissed, or replaced
*) choice=$(printf \(aq%s\en\(aq "$items" | fuzzel \-\-dmenu) ;;
esac
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
The compositor's socket, relative to
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
or absolute.
logis does not fall back to
.BR wayland\-0 .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the daemon's socket and log live; must be absolute.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration is looked for (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.B \-\-servitor
and plain
.BR logis 's
rites find
.BR servitor ;
the caller's, not the daemon's (from a compositor keybind, the
compositor's).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/logis.sock
The daemon's socket.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/logis.log
The diagnostics of a daemon a client started, once it has left the client's
stderr behind.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/logis/frecency.json
How often and how lately each application was launched.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/logis/tab
The tab plain
.BR logis 's
tabbed launcher was last used on:
.B applications
or
.BR rites .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/logis/logis.jsonc
The configuration (JSON with comments), unless
.B \-\-config
names another.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/logis/themes/NAME.jsonc
A theme the configuration's
.B theme
names, described by
.IR schema/theme.schema.json .
Its
.B effects
are groups of settings for the CRT pass over the panel (scanlines, glow,
aberration, mask, roundness, grain, flicker); a bare number is a group's
strength, and the older keys
.BR bloom ,
.B curvature
and
.B vignette
still set their groups' values.
Its
.B effects.glitch
configures a glitch pass before the CRT pass: parts
with an amount at rest and in an episode, ambient episodes at the end of each
period counted from the panel's opening, and bursts on opening, on a switch of
tab, on a key press (a small one:
.B key\-ms
long, peaking at
.BR key\-strength )
and at random; a bare number is its strength, and 0 or
.B reduce\-motion
turns it off.
Its
.B effects.custom\-shader
names a GLSL ES 3.00 fragment shader, a path from the
theme's file, that runs after the built-in effects: it defines
.BR mainImage ,
as for Shadertoy, and reads
.BR iChannel0 ,
.BR iResolution ,
.BR iTime ,
.B iFrame
and
.BR iGlitch .
The configuration's
.B custom\-shader
(a path from its directory) outranks it.
A shader that cannot be read or compiled is skipped with one line in the log;
.B \-\-check\-config
compiles it and names the line at fault.
